Let the vertices of the pentagon be $A = (-1, -1)$, $B = (-3, 4)$, $C = (1, 7)$, $D = (6, 5)$, and $E = (3, -1)$. We can find the area of the pentagon using the Shoelace Theorem.

The Shoelace Theorem states that the area of a polygon with vertices $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \dots, (x_n, y_n)$ listed in clockwise or counterclockwise order is
$$ \frac{1}{2} |(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_3 + \dots + x_{n-1} y_n + x_n y_1) - (y_1 x_2 + y_2 x_3 + \dots + y_{n-1} x_n + y_n x_1)| $$
Here, we have $A = (-1, -1)$, $B = (-3, 4)$, $C = (1, 7)$, $D = (6, 5)$, and $E = (3, -1)$.
\begin{align*} \text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} |(-1 \cdot 4 + (-3) \cdot 7 + 1 \cdot 5 + 6 \cdot (-1) + 3 \cdot (-1)) - (-1 \cdot (-3) + 4 \cdot 1 + 7 \cdot 6 + 5 \cdot 3 + (-1) \cdot (-1))| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |(-4 - 21 + 5 - 6 - 3) - (3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |(-29) - (65)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-29 - 65| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-94| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} (94) \\ &= 47 \end{align*}
Thus, the area of the pentagon is 47.

\boxed{47 \text{ square units}}
\boxed{47}

