We are given the vertices of a convex pentagon: $(-1, -1), (-3, 4), (1, 7), (6, 5),$ and $(3, -1)$.
We will use the Shoelace Theorem to find the area of the pentagon.

The Shoelace Theorem states that the area of a polygon with vertices $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \dots, (x_n, y_n)$ is given by
$$ A = \frac{1}{2} \left| \sum_{i=1}^n x_i y_{i+1} - \sum_{i=1}^n y_i x_{i+1} \right|, $$
where $x_{n+1} = x_1$ and $y_{n+1} = y_1$.

Let the vertices be $A(-1, -1)$, $B(-3, 4)$, $C(1, 7)$, $D(6, 5)$, and $E(3, -1)$. Then
\begin{align*} \sum_{i=1}^5 x_i y_{i+1} &= (-1)(4) + (-3)(7) + (1)(5) + (6)(-1) + (3)(-1) \\ &= -4 - 21 + 5 - 6 - 3 \\ &= -29 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} \sum_{i=1}^5 y_i x_{i+1} &= (-1)(-3) + (4)(1) + (7)(6) + (5)(3) + (-1)(-1) \\ &= 3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1 \\ &= 65 \end{align*}
Then the area is
$$ A = \frac{1}{2} | -29 - 65 | = \frac{1}{2} | -94 | = \frac{1}{2}(94) = 47 $$

Thus, the area of the pentagon is 47 square units.

\boxed{47 \text{ square units} }
\boxed{47}

